{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 15, :comments ["<b>Seven days.</b> Now, the reason is because their dough had not had time to become leavened, and the Redeemer redeemed them. And if you would say: but those who were departing had not yet departed to make a remembrance of the matter? Behold, even the paschal offering that HaShem commanded to take — a lamb per ancestral house, etc. — He had not yet passed over; rather, in the merit of this commandment HaShem would pass over. So too the matzah: in the merit of this commandment He would hasten to redeem them. And so it was." "<b>And you will find</b> that our Rabbis of blessed memory said (Pesachim 120a), [drawing] the derashot of the Torah by one of the thirteen hermeneutical rules: for all seven days there is no obligation of matzah except on the first night — Scripture fixed it as an obligation. And the reason is that the dimension of the commandment too existed only on the night of the fifteenth, and this was fixed as an obligation in its own time; from then onward the miracle bore no reference to the commandment. Understand this. And the reason it says ״שִׁבְעַת יָמִים מַצּוֹת״ is nothing but permission. Or perhaps it spoke in this order to say that Scripture credits him as though he ate matzah all seven days." "And you will find that HaShem commanded two sacred convocations — on the first day and on the seventh day: the first day, on account of the miracle of the fifteenth day; and the seventh day, on account of the miracle of the culmination of the redemption, which was the splitting of the Sea of Reeds, etc." "A further reason it says <b>שִׁבְעַת</b>: because HaShem did not command, for all seven days, a prohibition of ״you shall not eat leavened bread.״ I might then say: if one did not eat leavened bread, he has neither merit nor punishment. Scripture therefore says ״שִׁבְעַת יָמִים תֹּאכַל מַצּוֹת״ — to tell you that when one did not eat leavened bread it is as though he ate matzah, and so he has seven positive commandments in his hand." "And the reason for seven — neither fewer nor more — is that HaShem intended that they be occupied with commandments until the culmination of the redemption, which was on the seventh day of Pesach, when HaShem performed extraordinary miracles for them and judgments upon their pursuers." "There is also a reason in spirituality, for the foundations of holiness, which are the root of the souls of Israel, are seven eyes (Zechariah 3:9), and Knesset Israel is called Bat Sheva for this reason (Zohar vol. 3 6a)."], :book :exodus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 12, :notes {5 "The citation זהר חלק ג ו: appears to reference Zohar III (Vayikra/Emor section) folio 6, side unspecified; rendered as 6a by convention."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:27:09.393989605Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}